import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
---
<LegalLayout title="Community Guidelines">
  <h2>Our Commitment</h2>
  <p>
    Belongary exists to surface the truth about workplace belonging. That truth is only useful if the community maintains it honestly, respectfully, and in good faith. These guidelines define what we expect from every reviewer.
  </p>

  <h2>Review Standards</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Be honest.</strong> Report your genuine experience. Don't exaggerate, don't minimize, don't review a company you never worked at.</li>
    <li><strong>Be specific.</strong> Rate dimensions you can speak to. Skip ones you can't. Honest "I don't know" is more valuable than a guess.</li>
    <li><strong>Be respectful.</strong> Criticize systems, not individuals. "Management didn't support my growth" is fair. Naming your manager is not.</li>
    <li><strong>Be anonymous.</strong> Don't identify yourself or others. No names, no titles specific enough to identify someone, no dates precise enough to narrow down who you are.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Gets Rejected</h2>
  <p>
    Every review is read by a human moderator. The following will be rejected outright — not edited, not cleaned up, just rejected with a request to resubmit:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Content that names specific individuals</li>
    <li>Hate speech, slurs, or identity-based attacks</li>
    <li>Content specific enough to identify the reviewer or other employees</li>
    <li>Spam, marketing, or content clearly unrelated to workplace experience</li>
    <li>Reviews of companies the reviewer demonstrably did not work at</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>We never edit your words.</strong> If your review is rejected, you'll be asked to resubmit. What appears on Belongary is always written by the reviewer, in their own language, unmodified.
  </p>

  <h2>Lens Integrity</h2>
  <p>
    Identity lenses are self-reported and voluntary. We don't verify them and we can't police them. We trust the community to use them in good faith. If someone tags a review with a lens that doesn't apply to them, they're distorting the signal for people who need it — and we rely on volume and moderation to dilute that noise over time.
  </p>

  <h2>Disputes</h2>
  <p>
    Companies can dispute their scores through a formal process. Reviewers can request corrections or removal of their own reviews by contacting us at the email they provided during submission. See our <a href="/dispute" class="text-coral hover:text-coral/80 underline">Dispute Process</a> for details.
  </p>

  <h2>Anonymity</h2>
  <p>
    We protect your anonymity by not collecting your identity in the first place. Read how on our dedicated <a href="/anonymity" class="text-coral hover:text-coral/80 underline">anonymity page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Enforcement</h2>
  <p>
    We reserve the right to reject any review that violates these guidelines. Repeated violations from the same source may result in all associated reviews being removed. We don't publicly disclose moderation actions.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
